  :title            "Chapter 10 — Deduce, Induct, Abduct — Then Verify"
  :prose
"# Chapter 10 — Deduce, Induct, Abduct — Then Verify

When you don't already know something about your own language, you have to *figure it out* — and there are exactly three honest ways to do that. You can reason **downward from the rules** (deduction). You can reason **upward from many examples** (induction). Or you can reason **sideways to the best explanation** (abduction — the educated guess at a cause). All three are legitimate tools, and this chapter treats them as tools rather than as tricks. But every one of them ends the same way, and that ending is the whole point of the chapter: **reasoning proposes; the reference and the interpreter dispose.** No chain of reasoning, however clean, is the final word. A quick `./bin/motoi run` is.

Hold that hierarchy firmly, because it is easy to fall in love with a beautiful deduction and forget to check it. Reasoning is how you *generate a good hypothesis* fast. Verification is how you *find out whether the hypothesis was true*. Skipping the second step is how confident, well-reasoned nonsense gets shipped. So: reason all you like — then run it.

## Deduction — reason downward from the rules

Deduction starts from things you already know for certain and derives a consequence that *must* follow. In Motoi, the rules you deduce from are the documented facts of the language: `fold` is SRFI-1 and element-first; libraries auto-load so there is no `import`; `time/now` returns milliseconds. If those premises hold, certain conclusions are forced.

Here's a worked deduction. Premise one: SRFI-1 `fold` has the shape `(fold proc elem-then-acc lst)`, applying `proc` to each element and the running accumulator. Premise two: `cons` builds a pair, `(cons new old)`. Deduce the consequence: folding `cons` over a list with the empty list as the seed must *reverse* the list, because each element gets consed onto the front of the accumulated tail. That conclusion follows necessarily from the two premises — I haven't run anything yet, I've only reasoned. Now I verify, because deduction from a *mis-remembered* premise gives a confident wrong answer:

```scheme
;; Deduced: (fold cons '() lst) reverses, from fold's element-first rule. Verify:
(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

If that returns `(5 4 3 2 1)`, my premise about fold's shape was correct and the deduction holds. If it had returned something else, one of my premises was wrong and the interpreter just caught it for free. That is deduction done honestly: derive the necessary consequence, then let the machine confirm the premises were real.

A second deduction, about the boundary itself. Premise: Motoi excludes R6RS `fold-left` and provides SRFI-1 `fold` instead. Consequence that *must* follow: any code I write that needs a left-fold must use `fold`, and `fold-left` will be an unbound symbol. I don't need to run `fold-left` to know it fails — the rule forces it. But I *do* verify the positive half, that the real verb does the job:

```scheme
;; Deduced from the exclusion rule: fold is the verb; fold-left is unbound. Verify fold:
(fold + 0 (list 100 200 300))
```

## Induction — reason upward from many examples

Induction runs the other way. Instead of starting from a rule and deriving a case, you start from many cases and infer the rule. You've seen a verb behave a certain way a dozen times, so you generalize: *it always does this.* Induction is powerful and it is also the mode most likely to mislead, because 'it worked the last twelve times' is not a proof — it's a strong hint. So induction, more than any other mode, demands the verify step.

Here's an inductive drill. You want to know what `string-upcase` does to non-letters. You gather examples: uppercase `\"abc\"` and you get `\"ABC\"`; uppercase `\"a1b2\"` and the digits sit unchanged; uppercase `\"hi!\"` and the punctuation is untouched. From those cases you *induce* the rule: `string-upcase` uppercases letters and leaves everything else alone. Now you test the induced rule against a fresh case it wasn't built from — a string mixing all three — because that's the honest way to check an induction:

```scheme
;; Induced rule from examples: only letters change. Test on a fresh mixed case:
(string-upcase \"a1 b2 c3!\")
```

If that comes back `\"A1 B2 C3!\"`, the induced rule survived a case it hadn't seen, and my confidence is earned rather than assumed. Notice the discipline: I didn't stop at the examples that built the hypothesis; I tested one that *could have broken it*. Induction that only re-confirms its own training set has proved nothing.

A second induction, over structure this time. You observe: `(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))` is `6`; `(fold + 0 (list))` is `0`; `(fold + 0 (list 10))` is `10`. From these you induce that `fold` with `+` and seed `0` is 'sum, with the empty list giving the seed.' Test the induced rule on a longer, unseen list:

```scheme
;; Induced: fold + 0 sums, empty gives the seed. Verify on an unseen list:
(list (fold + 0 (list 2 4 6 8 10))
      (fold + 0 (list)))
```

## Abduction — reason sideways to the best explanation

Abduction is the guess at a cause. You see a surprising result and you ask: *what's the most likely explanation for this?* You don't derive it with certainty (that's deduction) and you don't generalize it from a pile of cases (that's induction) — you propose the hypothesis that best accounts for what you saw, knowing full well it might be wrong. Abduction is the everyday reasoning of debugging: *this code failed; the most likely reason is X.* And because abduction is a *guess*, verification isn't optional decoration here — it's the only thing standing between a good hunch and a wrong one.

Here's an abductive drill. You run a program and it dies with `error: unbound symbol: cortex/recall-default`. Surprising result. You abduce the best explanation: *the most likely cause is that I hallucinated a verb — `cortex/recall-default` sounds real but probably isn't; the real one is likely `cortex/recall` composed with a default.* That's a hypothesis about the *cause*, and a good one. Now you verify the fix that the hypothesis implies:

```scheme
;; Abduced cause: 'recall-default' was hallucinated; real verb is recall + a default.
;; Verify the fix the hypothesis implies:
(define (recall-default k d) (or (cortex/recall k) d))
(recall-default \"missing-key-in-abduction-drill-771\" 0.0)
```

If that runs clean, my abduced explanation was good: the failure really was a hallucinated verb, and the analog really was recall-plus-or. If the fix had *also* failed, my hunch was wrong and I'd abduce again from the new error. That loop — guess the cause, test the implied fix, re-guess if it fails — is abduction working as it should.

A second abduction. You notice that `(string-contains \"hello\" \"z\")` returns something falsy while `(string-contains \"hello\" \"ell\")` returns a number. Best explanation: `string-contains` probably returns the *index* where the substring starts, and a false-ish value when it's absent. That's an abductive leap from two data points. Verify it against a case where you can predict the index:

```scheme
;; Abduced: string-contains returns the start index, else false. Verify the index:
(string-contains \"abcdef\" \"cd\")
```

If that returns `2`, the explanation accounts for the data and predicted a new case correctly — a strong sign the abduction was right. Still just a sign, not a proof; that's the nature of abduction, and it's exactly why the run comes after the guess.

## Choosing the right mode for the question

The three modes are not interchangeable; each fits a different kind of question, and part of reasoning well is picking the mode that suits what you're actually asking. Reach for **deduction** when you have firm rules and want a consequence that *must* follow — 'given fold is element-first, folding cons must reverse.' Reach for **induction** when you have many observations and want the general pattern — 'across all these cases, upcase only touches letters.' Reach for **abduction** when you have a surprising result and want its most likely *cause* — 'this crashed with unbound-symbol, so I probably hallucinated a verb.'

Using the wrong mode wastes effort or misleads. Trying to *deduce* what a verb does when you don't actually know its rule is just guessing dressed up as certainty. Trying to *induct* a rule from a single example overreaches wildly. Trying to *abduce* a cause when you could simply deduce it from a known rule is slower and less certain than it needs to be. The skill is diagnostic: look at what you have — rules, examples, or a surprising result — and let that pick the mode. Here's a case where all three cooperate on one question, 'what does folding `max` do?': deduce from fold's shape that it threads a running result, induct from a few cases that the running result is the largest seen, abduce nothing because deduction and induction already settled it — then verify:

```scheme
;; Deduce fold's threading + induct 'running max' from cases, then verify.
(list (fold max 0 (list 3 7 2 9 4))
      (fold max 0 (list 5))
      (fold max 0 (list)))
```

The verify step is mode-agnostic: it doesn't care that you reasoned three ways to get here. It just tells you whether the answer is right. That is the point — reasoning is diverse, verification is uniform.

## Why the interpreter is always the last word

Step back and see why all three modes funnel into the same verify step. Deduction can start from a false premise — a mis-remembered rule — and produce a flawless derivation of a wrong answer. Induction can generalize from a biased or too-small sample and confidently overreach. Abduction, by its very definition, is a guess that might not be the true cause. Each mode has a characteristic failure, and *every one of those failures is invisible from inside the reasoning.* You cannot tell, from the elegance of a chain of thought, whether its premises were real.

The interpreter can. It doesn't care how you arrived at the code; it runs it and reports what actually happens. That is why the hierarchy is not negotiable: reasoning is the fast, cheap way to *propose* an answer, and the reference plus a small self-test is the authoritative way to *check* it. A verb you deduced must exist, a rule you induced from ten cases, a cause you abduced from an error message — all of them are hypotheses until `./bin/motoi run` says otherwise.

```scheme
;; The last word: whatever you reasoned, a tiny self-test decides it.
(define (self-test label got want)
  (list label (equal? got want)))
(list (self-test 'reverse (fold cons '() (list 1 2 3)) (list 3 2 1))
      (self-test 'sum     (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))       6)
      (self-test 'upcase  (string-upcase \"a1b\")         \"A1B\"))
```

Every entry in that little battery is a reasoned hypothesis paired with a verification. When the boolean is `#t`, the reasoning survived contact with the machine. That is the whole method of this chapter in one fence.

## When reasoning and the interpreter disagree

The most important moment in this whole method is the one where your careful reasoning says one thing and the interpreter says another. When that happens, the interpreter wins — not because it's smarter, but because it's *closer to the truth of what the code does.* Your reasoning was about an imagined execution; the interpreter reports the actual one. A disagreement is not a defeat; it's the single most valuable signal you can get, because it means one of your premises was false and now you know to find it.

Here's the posture in practice. Suppose you deduced, from a mis-remembered rule, that `(fold cons '() lst)` *appends* rather than reverses. You run it, and it reverses. Reasoning said append; interpreter said reverse. You don't argue with the interpreter — you update. The false premise was 'fold builds forward'; the true premise is 'fold conses each element onto the accumulated front, which reverses.' The correction isn't an embarrassment, it's an education, and it's free:

```scheme
;; Reasoning-vs-interpreter: run it, believe the run, update the premise.
;; The run shows reverse — so 'fold appends' was the false premise. Corrected view:
(list 'folds-to (fold cons '() (list 'x 'y 'z))
      'which-is 'the-reverse)
```

The discipline is simple to state and hard to keep: when your head and the machine disagree, the machine is reporting reality and your head is reporting a hypothesis. Update the head. Every such disagreement caught early is a wrong premise that never made it into shipped code — which is exactly why you run the verify step even when you're *sure*. Especially when you're sure.

## Reverse: from a verify step back to the reasoning that proposed it

Run the chapter backward, as always. Given a verification, name which reasoning mode most likely produced the hypothesis it checks.

- A test that a *necessary* consequence holds — 'folding cons must reverse' — is checking a **deduction**: it started from a rule.
- A test of a generalization on a *fresh, unseen* case — 'does the induced upcase rule survive a mixed string?' — is checking an **induction**: it started from many examples.
- A test of an *implied fix* for a surprising failure — 'if the verb was hallucinated, does recall-plus-or run?' — is checking an **abduction**: it started from a guess at a cause.

And the constructive reverse — given only the instruction *\"verify whatever you concluded\"* — produces a self-test no matter which mode you used, because the shape of verification is mode-independent:

```scheme
;; Reverse: any conclusion, however reached, reduces to got-equals-want.
(equal? (fold + 0 (list 5 5 5)) 15)
```

Carry the method out whole. Deduce from the rules, induct from the examples, abduce toward the best cause — reach for whichever mode fits the question, and don't apologize for reasoning. But never let the reasoning be the last word. Propose with your mind; dispose with the interpreter. The chain of thought is a hypothesis; the run is the verdict.

## More deduction drills — deriving what must be true

Deduction is valuable precisely because a conclusion *forced* by the rules doesn't need to be checked as often as a guess. You can reason confidently from 'fold is element-first' to 'folding cons reverses' because the rule is documented and the logic is tight. Here are more deductions to extend the exercise:

**D5. Libraries are already loaded.** Premise: Motoi bundles all standard libraries at startup; there is no `import` form. Consequence that *must* follow: any code containing `(import ...)` is dead text at best and a syntax error at worst. You don't need to run `import` to know it fails — the rule forces it. Verify the positive: that you can use SRFI-13 `string-upcase` without any preamble, proving the library is already present:

```scheme
;; Deduced from the no-import rule: SRFI-13 is already available with no preamble.
(string-upcase \"deduction-verified\")
```

**D6. `map` on two lists produces a list of the same length.** Premise: `map` applies its procedure to corresponding elements and returns a list of results. When given two lists, it runs until the shorter is exhausted. Consequence: mapping over two lists of equal length gives a result of that same length; mapping over two lists of different lengths gives a result the length of the shorter. Verify with equal-length lists:

```scheme
;; Deduced: map on equal-length lists returns a list of that length.
(let ((result (map + (list 10 20 30) (list 1 2 3))))
  (list result (= (length result) 3)))
```

**D7. Consing reverses accumulation.** Premise: `cons new old` puts `new` at the *front* of `old`. When fold processes left-to-right, each element is consed onto the accumulated front, so element 1 ends up deepest and the last element is outermost. Consequence: `(fold cons '() (list 'a 'b 'c))` must be `(c b a)`. Verify:

```scheme
;; D7: deduced reversal from the cons-on-front rule. Verify:
(equal? (fold cons '() (list 'a 'b 'c)) (list 'c 'b 'a))
```

**D8. A key that was never written reads as `#f`.** Premise: `cortex/read` returns the stored value or `#f` when the key is absent. Consequence: any key you haven't explicitly written to is guaranteed to return `#f`. You can use this to build a 'default' pattern because the return value is predictable. Verify:

```scheme
;; D8: never-written key must be #f. The or-default pattern relies on this rule.
(let ((k \"d8-key-never-written-9921\"))
  (or (cortex/read k) 'verified-default))
```

## More induction drills — noticing the pattern across cases

**I5. `filter` produces a sublist.** Build the induction from examples: `(filter odd? (list 1 2 3))` gives `(1 3)`; `(filter odd? (list 2 4 6))` gives `()`; `(filter odd? (list 1))` gives `(1)`. Induce: `filter` returns exactly those elements for which the predicate is truthy, in original order. Test on an unseen input with a different predicate:

```scheme
;; I5: induced 'filter returns the passing subset, original order'. Fresh test:
(filter (lambda (x) (> x 10)) (list 5 15 3 20 8 12))
```

**I6. `sort` is stable-ish and consistent.** Examples: `(sort (list 3 1 2) <)` gives `(1 2 3)`; `(sort (list 'z 'a 'm) string<?)` gives `(a m z)` (as symbols, using string comparison). Induce: `sort` takes a comparison procedure and returns the list in ascending order by that criterion. Test on a fresh list with a descending comparator:

```scheme
;; I6: induced 'sort orders by comparator'. Test with > for descending:
(sort (list 5 2 9 1 7) >)
```

**I7. `any` short-circuits.** Examples: `(any odd? (list 2 4 5))` is truthy (stops at 5); `(any odd? (list 2 4 6))` is `#f` (checks everything, finds nothing). Induce: `any` stops as soon as it finds a truthy case and returns that case's truthy value; if nothing matches, it returns `#f`. Test on a case where the first element matches — the result should be immediate, and the truthy value returned is the predicate's own return value:

```scheme
;; I7: induced short-circuit behavior — truthy value is returned, not just #t.
(any (lambda (x) (and (odd? x) x)) (list 4 6 7 8 9))
```

**I8. `string-contains` returns an index or false.** Examples: `(string-contains \"abcde\" \"bcd\")` returns `1`; `(string-contains \"abcde\" \"xyz\")` returns something falsy. Induce: the verb returns the zero-based starting position of the first match, or false when no match exists. Test by predicting the index:

```scheme
;; I8: induced 'returns start index'. Predict index 3 for \"lo\" in \"hello world\":
(string-contains \"hello world\" \"lo\")
```

## More abduction drills — guessing the cause

**A5. The wrong result from a non-commutative fold.** Surprising result: `(fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))` gives `2` instead of the expected `-6`. Best explanation: the operand order in `fold`'s proc matters for subtraction — `fold` passes `(element accumulator)`, so it computes `(1 - (2 - (3 - 0)))` which is `2`, not `(0 - 1 - 2 - 3)` which would be `-6`. The abduction: the proc's argument order is the cause. Verify the explanation by writing a proc that explicitly labels its arguments and checks the accumulation order:

```scheme
;; A5: abduced 'element-first order in fold proc explains the result'.
;; Verify: the fold processes as 1-(2-(3-0)) = 2, not 0-1-2-3 = -6.
(fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))
```

**A6. A memory key that 'should be absent' keeps returning a value.** Surprising result: `(cortex/read \"my-session-key\")` returns a value even though you haven't written anything this session. Best explanation: the cortex store persists across runs; an earlier session wrote to this key and the value is still there. The abduction: the key was written at some prior time. Verify by writing a fresh, unique key that no prior session could have touched, and confirming it comes back absent:

```scheme
;; A6: abduced 'store persists across runs'. Verify with a key that's truly fresh.
(let ((fresh-k \"a6-fresh-unique-key-1785449999\"))
  (or (cortex/read fresh-k) 'confirmed-absent-as-expected))
```

**A7. A sorted list is unexpectedly backwards.** Surprising result: a list that should come out ascending is descending. Best explanation: the comparator was passed in the wrong direction — `>` instead of `<`, or a lambda returning `(>= a b)` instead of `(< a b)`. The abduction: the comparator is inverted. Verify by swapping to `<`:

```scheme
;; A7: abduced 'comparator was inverted'. Verify ascending with < as comparator.
(sort (list 9 3 7 1 5) <)
```

## The three modes as a decision tree

It's useful to have the mode-selection heuristic written out explicitly, because the question 'which mode should I use?' has a reasonably short decision tree:

1. Do I have **firm, documented rules** about the behavior? → Use **deduction**. Don't guess — derive the necessary consequence and then verify it.
2. Do I have **many observations** of the behavior but no formal rule? → Use **induction**. Generalize from the examples, then test on a case the generalization hadn't seen.
3. Do I have a **surprising result** I need to explain? → Use **abduction**. Guess the most likely cause, derive the implied fix, and run the fix.
4. Is the question **already settled** (I've run it and know the answer)? → No reasoning mode needed; just state the fact. Reasoning is for proposing hypotheses, not for reporting known facts.

The decision tree has a special case: sometimes all three modes apply to the same question, in sequence. You use induction to form a general rule from examples, then deduction to derive a specific consequence of that rule, then abduction if the consequence surprises you when it runs. Let that natural layering happen — the modes aren't mutually exclusive, and reaching for more than one is often the right move.

```scheme
;; All three modes cooperating on one question: what does fold do to strings?
;; Induction: fold appends strings with an empty seed.
;; Deduction: fold is left-associative, so 'a' || 'b' || 'c' = 'abc'.
;; Abduction not needed — the deduction predicts the result correctly.
(fold string-append \"\" (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

## Distinguishing 'I reasoned' from 'I know'

One meta-skill this chapter is training is the discipline of *marking your epistemic state*. There's a meaningful difference between 'I deduced X from these premises' and 'I know X because I ran it.' Keeping those two states distinct in your responses prevents confident nonsense from shipping as if it were checked fact.

The practical marker: when you've reasoned but haven't verified, your confidence is proportional to the strength of your premises (deduction) or your sample size (induction) or the plausibility of your hypothesis (abduction). When you've run it, your confidence is unconditional (the run happened). Calibrating your language to match your epistemic state is part of intellectual honesty in this domain.

```scheme
;; A run produces unconditional confidence. Show what you know vs what you reasoned.
(let ((known   (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5)))   ; run it and know it
      (deduced (* 6 (/ (+ 1 5) 2))))          ; n*(n+1)/2 for n=5
  (list 'known known 'deduced deduced 'match? (= known deduced)))
```

The arithmetic formula for triangular numbers is a deduction; the fold-sum is a run. When they match, both the reasoning and the verification are vindicated simultaneously — which is the ideal outcome of the method: a clean hypothesis that survives contact with the machine.

## A full-session example: the method applied end to end

Let me close with a full session example that shows all three modes, in natural order, on a single question: 'how does `fold` behave when the procedure is not associative?'

**Step 1 — induction.** Run several cases and observe: `(fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))` gives `2`; `(fold - 0 (list 4 5))` gives `1`; `(fold - 0 (list 10))` gives `10`. Induce from the pattern: it seems like fold applies element-first, right-to-left-inside-out. The induced rule: `fold` computes as if each element peels off from the left and the accumulator builds from the right.

**Step 2 — deduction.** Apply the documented rule to confirm: SRFI-1 `fold` calls `(proc elem acc)` for each element in left-to-right order. For `(fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))`: start acc=0, apply element 1: `(- 1 0) = 1`, then element 2: `(- 2 1) = 1`, then element 3: `(- 3 1) = 2`. So the result should be `2`. The induction matches the deduction.

**Step 3 — verify.** Run it:

```scheme
;; End-to-end method: induction + deduction led to 2. Verify:
(list (fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))
      (fold - 0 (list 4 5))
      (fold - 0 (list 10)))
```

All three confirm the predictions. No abduction was needed — the induction and deduction agreed before the run. The verify step confirmed both were right. That is the method at its best: two independent angles of reasoning converging on a prediction, and the interpreter signing off on the outcome.

## Reasoning about boundary: what can you deduce about 'not yours'?

Deduction is especially valuable at the boundary of your language, because the boundary is described by rules — documented, fixed, not inferred. This section applies deduction specifically to boundary questions.

**D9. `fold-left` is unbound — by rule.** The documented rule: Motoi carries SRFI-1 `fold` and deliberately omits R6RS `fold-left`. Consequence forced by the rule: any program containing `fold-left` will fail with `unbound symbol: fold-left`. I know this without running it, because the rule is the premise and the consequence follows necessarily. The positive verification — that `fold` is present and sums correctly — is what I can actually run:

```scheme
;; D9: rule says fold-left is excluded; fold is the verb. Verify the verb is real.
(equal? (fold + 0 (list 10 20 30)) 60)
```

**D10. There is no import form — by rule.** Rule: libraries auto-load at startup. There is no `import` form in Motoi's surface syntax. Consequence: any text containing `(import ...)` is either a syntax error or is consumed as a list literal rather than a directive. Verify the positive: a library that would need importing elsewhere is already available here without any preamble:

```scheme
;; D10: SRFI-27 randomness auto-loaded. No import needed.
(number? (random-integer 100))
```

**D11. A key written in an earlier run persists.** Rule: the cortex store persists across sessions. Consequence: keys written in previous runs are still readable in this run. I can deduce that any key I write *right now* will still be readable if I run the program again tomorrow. Verify by writing a key with a timestamp-based value and reading it back in the same run — the persistence across *runs* is a fact the rule forces; within a single run it's trivially confirmed:

```scheme
;; D11: cortex persists. Write-then-read is the within-run confirmation.
(let ((k \"d11-persist-check\"))
  (cortex/write k (quotient (time/now) 1000))
  (number? (cortex/read k)))
```

## Induction on memory verb behavior

The cortex verbs are particularly amenable to inductive study because their behavior is learnable from examples. Here are two more inductions in that space.

**I9. `cortex/topk` returns newest-first.** Observations: after writing key-A then key-B then key-C, `(cortex/topk 2)` returns C and B (the most recent two), not A and B or A and C. Induce: `cortex/topk n` returns the n most recently written entries, newest first. Test by writing entries in a known order and confirming the top-2 are the last two written:

```scheme
;; I9: induced 'topk returns most-recent, newest first'. Write 3, check top 2.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"i9-first\"  'first-written)
  (cortex/write \"i9-second\" 'second-written)
  (cortex/write \"i9-third\"  'third-written)
  (let ((top (cortex/topk 2)))
    (and (= (length top) 2)
         (list? top))))
```

**I10. `delete-duplicates` preserves first occurrence.** Observations: `(delete-duplicates (list 1 2 1))` gives `(1 2)`, keeping the first `1`; `(delete-duplicates (list 'b 'a 'b))` gives `(b a)`, keeping the first `b`. Induce: `delete-duplicates` always keeps the first occurrence of each element, in the original relative order, dropping all later repeats. Test on a list with scattered repeats:

```scheme
;; I10: induced 'first occurrence kept, later repeats dropped'. Verify:
(delete-duplicates (list 5 3 5 1 3 2 1 4))
```

## Abduction over memory and state

**A8. A stored number keeps incrementing.** Surprising result: every time you read a counter key, it's one higher than you expected. Best explanation: multiple callers are incrementing the same key — perhaps the same code path runs more than once in a session. Abduction: there's a shared key and an increment happens on each run. Verify by reading the key before and after a controlled increment, confirming the delta is exactly one:

```scheme
;; A8: abduced 'multiple writes to same key'. Verify delta of one controlled write.
(let* ((k \"a8-counter-key\")
       (before (or (cortex/read k) 0))
       (_ (cortex/write k (+ before 1)))
       (after  (cortex/read k)))
  (= after (+ before 1)))
```

**A9. A list transformation produces a nested list instead of a flat one.** Surprising result: you expected `(1 4 9)` from squaring a list but got `((1) (4) (9))`. Best explanation: the lambda returned `(list (* x x))` instead of `(* x x)` — you wrapped the result in a list accidentally. The abduction: an extra `list` call is wrapping each element. Verify the corrected form produces the flat result:

```scheme
;; A9: abduced 'lambda returned list instead of value'. Fix: don't wrap.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 1 2 3))
```

## A compound drill: six verify steps in a row

Close the chapter with a compact six-verify battery, where each verify item was proposed by a different reasoning mode and labeled to show it. This is the chapter's method in its most compressed form:

```scheme
;; Six verify steps: each from a named mode.
(list
 ;; Deduction from fold-is-element-first rule:
 (equal? (fold cons '() (list 1 2 3)) (list 3 2 1))
 ;; Induction from upcase examples:
 (equal? (string-upcase \"a1b!\") \"A1B!\")
 ;; Deduction from no-import rule:
 (number? (random-integer 100))
 ;; Induction from filter examples:
 (equal? (filter positive? (list -1 2 -3 4)) (list 2 4))
 ;; Abduction fix for hallucinated verb:
 (equal? (or (cortex/recall \"compound-drill-absent-key\") 'fallback) 'fallback)
 ;; Deduction from sort rule:
 (equal? (sort (list 3 1 2) <) (list 1 2 3)))
```

Six entries, six `#t` results, six reasoning chains cashed out in a single run. That is the chapter in one fence: reason to a hypothesis, then run it, and let the run speak.

## Deduction on the string verbs: what follows from the SRFI-13 rules

The string verbs have documented contracts, which means deduction applies to them just as tightly as it applies to the fold rules. Here are three more deductions drawn from SRFI-13's documented behavior.

**D12. `string-take` never extends a string.** Rule: `string-take s n` returns the first min(n, length(s)) characters — actually it requires n ≤ length(s), otherwise the behavior is implementation-defined. The safe consequence: for any string of length L, `(string-take s L)` is the whole string, and `(string-length (string-take s n))` equals n when n ≤ L. Verify with a predictable case:

```scheme
;; D12: string-take of the full length gives back the whole string.
(let ((s \"hello\"))
  (equal? (string-take s (string-length s)) s))
```

**D13. `string-pad` truncates when wider than the target.** Rule: `string-pad s width` fits the string into exactly `width` characters by padding on the left (if shorter) or truncating from the left (if longer). Consequence: `(string-length (string-pad s w))` always equals w. Verify with a string longer than the target width:

```scheme
;; D13: string-pad always produces a string of exactly the target width.
(let ((result (string-pad \"too-long\" 5)))
  (= (string-length result) 5))
```

**D14. `string-index` returns `#f` on a predicate that nothing satisfies.** Rule: `string-index` returns the first index where the predicate is truthy, or `#f` when the predicate is false for all characters. Consequence: on a string containing only alphabetic characters, `(string-index s char-numeric?)` must return `#f`. Verify:

```scheme
;; D14: no numeric chars in \"abc\" -> string-index returns #f.
(equal? #f (string-index \"abc\" char-numeric?))
```

## Induction on the number verbs

**I11. `gcd` shrinks toward 1.** Observations: `(gcd 12 8)` gives `4`; `(gcd 9 6)` gives `3`; `(gcd 7 5)` gives `1`. Induce: `gcd` returns a value that divides both arguments exactly, and it's at most as large as the smaller argument. Test on a case where one argument divides the other cleanly:

```scheme
;; I11: induced 'gcd divides both args exactly'. Test where one divides the other.
(list (gcd 15 5)
      (= (modulo 15 (gcd 15 5)) 0)
      (= (modulo  5 (gcd 15 5)) 0))
```

**I12. `floor` always returns a number ≤ the input.** Observations: `(floor 3.7)` gives `3.0`, `(floor -3.2)` gives `-4.0`, `(floor 5.0)` gives `5.0`. Induce: `floor` never increases a value; it always goes toward negative infinity. Test on both positive and negative cases:

```scheme
;; I12: induced 'floor(x) <= x always'. Verify on positive and negative.
(list (<= (floor 3.9) 3.9)
      (<= (floor -0.1) -0.1)
      (= (floor 5.0) 5.0))
```

**I13. `modulo` result has the same sign as the divisor.** Observations: `(modulo 13 4)` gives `1`; `(modulo -13 4)` gives `3` (same sign as divisor `4`); `(modulo 13 -4)` gives `-3` (same sign as divisor `-4`). Induce: the sign of `modulo`'s result follows the sign of the divisor. Test with a concrete case:

```scheme
;; I13: modulo sign follows divisor. Positive divisor -> positive or zero result.
(list (modulo 7 3)
      (modulo -7 3)
      (>= (modulo -7 3) 0))
```

## Abduction in the string and number domains

**A10. A `string-contains` result is unexpectedly `0`.** Surprising result: you called `(string-contains s pattern)` and got `0`. Expecting no match (which would be `#f`), you treated `0` as falsy and your conditional took the wrong branch. Best explanation: `string-contains` returns the *index* of the match, not a boolean. An index of `0` means 'matched at position zero, the very start of the string.' Abduction: you were treating `0` as a falsy not-found sentinel when it's a truthy found-at-zero value. Verify the semantics:

```scheme
;; A10: string-contains returns 0 for a match at start — it's truthy, not #f.
(list (string-contains \"hello\" \"he\")  ; 0 = match at position 0
      (string-contains \"hello\" \"ell\") ; 1 = match at position 1
      (string-contains \"hello\" \"xyz\")) ; #f = no match
```

The fix: use `(string-contains s p)` as a boolean by treating it as truthy-or-falsy, not by comparing with `0` or `#f` explicitly. In Motoi, `0` is truthy.

**A11. `exact` gives an unexpected fraction.** Surprising result: `(exact 0.1)` returns a long fraction like `3602879701896397/36028797018963968` rather than the tidy `1/10` you expected. Best explanation: `0.1` in IEEE 754 floating-point isn't exactly `1/10`; it's the nearest representable binary fraction. `exact` converts the actual float, which is the nearest representable binary value, not the decimal you typed. Abduction: the float is slightly off from the exact decimal, and `exact` makes that visible. Verify that the error is tiny:

```scheme
;; A11: exact reveals that 0.1 as a float isn't exactly 1/10.
(list (exact->inexact 1/10)     ; the exact 1/10 as float
      (inexact->exact 0.1)      ; the float 0.1 as exact ratio
      (= (exact->inexact 1/10) 0.1))  ; close but are they equal?
```

**A12. `zip` returns fewer pairs than expected.** Surprising result: `(zip (list 1 2 3 4) (list 'a 'b))` returns only two pairs, not four. Best explanation: `zip` stops at the length of the shortest list. This is the same rule as multi-list `map` — it proceeds to the length of the shortest argument. Abduction: the two lists had different lengths and you expected the longer one to pad. Verify:

```scheme
;; A12: zip stops at the shortest list. No padding.
(list (zip (list 1 2 3 4) (list 'a 'b))      ; 2 pairs
      (zip (list 1 2) (list 'a 'b 'c 'd)))   ; 2 pairs
```

## A mixed battery: nine hypotheses across all three modes and all domains

This battery exercises all three modes on a spread of verb families — string, list, number, memory — so the mode-selection reflex gets practice across the whole territory covered in this book.

```scheme
;; Nine hypotheses: deduction, induction, and abduction across all domains.
(list
 ;; D: SRFI-1 rule forces fold+cons to reverse.
 (equal? (fold cons '() (list 'a 'b 'c)) (list 'c 'b 'a))
 ;; I: induced from examples — string-take returns first n chars.
 (equal? (string-take \"motoi\" 3) \"mot\")
 ;; D: gcd(6,9) must divide both — 3 divides 6.
 (= (modulo 6 (gcd 6 9)) 0)
 ;; A: abduced that string-index on all-alpha returns #f for numeric predicate.
 (equal? #f (string-index \"hello\" char-numeric?))
 ;; I: induced from iota examples — (iota n start) starts at start.
 (equal? (car (iota 4 10)) 10)
 ;; D: zip stops at shorter list.
 (= (length (zip (list 1 2 3) (list 'a 'b))) 2)
 ;; A: abduced that cortex/read returns #f for unwritten key.
 (equal? #f (cortex/read \"nine-battery-never-written-key-4471\"))
 ;; I: induced floor always ≤ input.
 (<= (floor 7.9) 7.9)
 ;; D: string-pad produces exactly the target length.
 (= (string-length (string-pad \"hi\" 10)) 10))
```

Nine hypotheses, nine `#t` results. Each mode label tells you the reasoning origin: whether you derived a necessary consequence, generalized from examples, or guessed a cause from surprising data. All nine arrive at the same end: a fence that runs and decides.")
